  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIt's a complex cloud cover forecast this evening, tonight and into \nTuesday. There are  strato-cumulus clouds developing in the lower \nlevels of the atmosphere streaming in from the northeast over the \narea. These should start to wane toward sunset. At the same time mid \nlevel clouds are trying to push in from the southwest, but are being \ndeflected by the high pressure to the north. The latest blend of \nCAMs and other global guidance still suggests mostly cloudy to \novercast conditions over the area most of tonight. Toward daybreak \nbreak early Tuesday morning, the clouds should clear across the \nnorthern half to third of Central NY. Strato cumulus clouds then \nlikely redevelop as some modest daytime heating takes place across \nCentral NY. Meanwhile NE PA, and especially the Wyoming Valley area \nwill see thicker stratus clouds overnight and into Tuesday, along \nwith some periods of light rain at times. Rainfall amounts continue \nto decrease, now expected 0.10 inches or lower only along the I-80 \ncorridor in Luzerne County...elsewhere measurable rain is not \nexpected. Temperatures will be cool tonight, dropping into the 40s \nfor most locations (low 50s Wyoming Valley).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThere remains high confidence in dry, partly sunny and seasonably \nmild weather Tuesday afternoon through Thursday across the region. A \nstrong surface high remains anchored to our north and east over \nsouthern Quebec and an upper level ridge moves over the area by \nWednesday (out ahead of a cut off 500mb low over the Central Great \nLakes/Ohio Valley). This setup keeps a northeasterly flow of dry and \ncool air advecting into the forecast area. By later Thursday an \nupper level low phases together with northern stream energy to \ndevelop a 500mb low over Virginia, but again our area remains dry. \nTemperatures will be in the 60s for highs each day, with lows in the \nupper 30s to 40s each night. If skies clear enough overnight there \ncertainly could be patchy fog and even patchy frost, especially by \nWednesday night, but only moderate confidence on this occurring.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nModel guidance continues to struggle with the complex weather \npattern setting up Friday into the weekend. As mentioned above some \nupper level energy will dive south across New England Thursday and \nmerge with the 500mb low drifting east from the Ohio Valley into the \nMid-Atlantic. 12z deterministic guidance such as the GFS and ECMWF \nhave now shifted further south and east with the coastal low track. \nConsidering all the uncertainty with this system, taking an ensemble \napproach was best to account for difference in the different deterministic\nmodel guidance. As the low potentially wraps up and strengthens\nalong the northern Mid-Atlantic coast it could still send some \nperiods of rain back into our area from the east. The official \nforecast maintains a slight chance to low end chance for showers\nFriday into Saturday, mainly along and east of I-81. Trends in \nthe latest guidance will continue to be monitored closely in the\ncoming days as we attempt to resolve this storms track and \npotential impacts here locally. At this time, the possible outcomes\nrange from mostly sunny and dry conditions Friday/Saturday...to\ncooler and cloudy with periods of rain. North-northwest winds will\nalso be breezy and gusty, 8-15 mph with gusts up to 20-25 mph \nin this timeframe.",
